609: The `--default()` function can also be used inside `--modifier()` to provide a default value when no modifier is present:
610: 
611: ```css
612: /* [!code filename:CSS] */
613: /* [!code word:--default(1)] */
614: @utility tab-* {
615:   tab-size: --value(integer);
616:   line-height: --modifier(integer, --default(1));
617: }
618: ```
619: 
620: This matches `tab-2/3` with a line-height of `3`, and `tab-2` with a line-height of `1`.
621: 
622: #### Negative values
623: 
624: To support negative values, register separate positive and negative utilities into separate declarations:
625: 
626: ```css
627: /* [!code filename:CSS] */
628: @utility inset-* {
629:   inset: --spacing(--value(integer));
630:   inset: --value([percentage], [length]);
631: }
632: 
633: /* [!code highlight:4] */
634: @utility -inset-* {
635:   inset: --spacing(--value(integer) * -1);
636:   inset: calc(--value([percentage], [length]) * -1);
637: }
638: ```
639: 
640: #### Modifiers
641: 
642: Modifiers are handled using the `--modifier()` function which works exactly like the `--value()` function but operates on a modifier if present:
643: 
644: ```css
645: /* [!code filename:CSS] */
646: @utility text-* {
647:   /* prettier-ignore */
648:   font-size: --value(--text-*, [length]);
649:   /* [!code highlight:2] */
650:   /* prettier-ignore */
651:   line-height: --modifier(--leading-*, [length], [*]);
652: }
653: ```
654: 
655: If a modifier isn't present, any declaration depending on a modifier is just not included in the output.
656: 
657: #### Fractions
658: 
659: To handle fractions, we rely on the CSS `ratio` data type. If this is used with `--value()`, it's a signal to Tailwind to treat the value and modifier as a single value:
660: 
661: ```css
662: /* [!code filename:CSS] */
663: @utility aspect-* {
664:   /* [!code word:ratio, \[ratio\]] */
665:   /* prettier-ignore */
666:   aspect-ratio: --value(--aspect-ratio-*, ratio, [ratio]);
667: }
668: ```
669: 
670: This will match utilities like `aspect-square`, `aspect-3/4`, and `aspect-[7/9]`.
671: 
672: ## Adding custom variants
673: 
674: In addition to using the variants that ship with Tailwind, you can also add your own custom variants using the `@custom-variant` directive:
675: 
676: ```css
677: @custom-variant theme-midnight {
678:   &:where([data-theme="midnight"] *) {
679:     @slot;
680:   }
681: }
682: ```
683: 
684: Now you can use the `theme-midnight:<utility>` variant in your HTML:
685: 
686: ```html
687: <!-- [!code classes:theme-midnight:bg-black] -->
688: <html data-theme="midnight">
689:   <button class="theme-midnight:bg-black ..."></button>
690: </html>
691: ```
692: 
693: You can create variants using the shorthand syntax when nesting isn't required:
694: 
695: ```css
696: @custom-variant theme-midnight (&:where([data-theme="midnight"] *));
697: ```
698: 
699: When a custom variant has multiple rules, they can be nested within each other:

